  "conclusions": {
    "rigorous_bounds_economic_failure": "Rigorous upper bounds (E1 operator norm and E2 barrier majorant) provide provable zero underestimation, but their extreme conservativeness (O(N) = O(1/h^2) scaling) causes them to reject 100.0% of queries under the MaxAE <= 10 K engineering specification across all resolutions (32x32 to 256x256). Because they add certificate evaluation overhead before escalating all queries to FVM, their realized speedup is <= 0.6x (slower than direct FVM alone). Mathematically certified acceptance of neural operator surrogates is therefore economically and operationally infeasible.",
    "numerical_estimator_performance": "Preconditioned Jacobi residual gating (P4) and Selective Jacobi smoothing + residual gating (P5) achieve substantial unsafe reduction: at 32x32, P5 accepts 71.0% of cases while holding CFR_10K to 4.2% and achieving 1.1x speedup. However, at 256x256, because the underlying surrogate error distribution has mean MaxAE = 13.7 K (80% unsafe), filtering for safe cases forces escalation rates above 85%, reducing the net realized speedup to ~1.1x to 1.3x.",
    "production_recommendation": "Neither rigorous error certificates nor numerical residual estimators can transform a surrogate with an 80% baseline violation rate into a certified safe production hybrid with high speedup. Production promotion must be REJECTED. The system must remain on the frozen R1 direct FVM pipeline."
  }
